ned software  engineer just like countless examples  that I saw in the industry and what's  common amongst all these different  people were that they were passionate  they were determined and they were smart  and at that end they just wanted this  really badly and they figured out a way  to do this without the degree so I was  like okay if these people can do it I  can do it too  right and I think when you're kind of  making this decision it's important to  just be honest with yourself and so when  I look back and think through why I  think I might have been successful  without the degree is you know there's a  few different reasons I can think of one  is that I kind of already had a  technical background so in college I was  a systems engineer  and I you know because of that I took a  lot of pretty heavy high-level math and  engineering courses so I already like I  was already kind of trained to think by  analytically it was very good with math  and very good with numbers but that's  not to say that you need a technical  degree to be a developer I think I know  plenty of people who studied like  philosophy or psychology in college and  then later on in life realized they want  to be developers but for me like I think  that was one of the reasons why I was  pretty successful in this transition and  second I was obviously extremely  passionate about it and and I kind of  conveyed this passion throughout various  blog posts ever in and I just wanted  this so badly and I think because I  wanted so badly I was incredibly  determined I worked really hard and I  put I put a lot a lot of time and effort  into making this reality happen and you  know along the way I did have to give up  and sacrifice a lot like for example I  didn't see my family for almost a year  during the process I didn't see a lot of  my friends for many many months I was  pretty much doing this day out day in  and day out just learning how to code  every single day getting a little bit  better at it and even you know even my  boyfriend I didn't see for life you know  I saw him once a week which is like  really really hard obviously but you  kind of I was kind of willing to give up  these things because I knew that I  wanted this dream to come true so badly  and I think this might motivate some  people so early on in my decision  process I was talking to a really good  mentor and you know he was he's this  kind of highly academic person who has  the C s degree and he has a PhD in  computer science you know went to  Stanford so very academic and I was kind  of telling him about my decision to go  become a software developer and I was  kind of saying like hey like I don't  know like one thing I'm worried about is  how will I ever catch up with these  people who've been programming since  they were 12 years old they literally  have like a decade of experience over me  it may be even a college degree and what  he said he looked at me in the eye and  he's like what I see in you is great and  passion you're gonna get there in no  time just just go for it  and when someone like that said that to  me someone whose academic it has a  degree and he just like he's like no you  can do it without it like just go figure  it out it's all about just doing and  that really was inspiring to me and I  was like okay I can do this without the  degree and kind of to address his  question of you know the industry is  being flooded with junior developers  that don't know enough to be productive  I totally I've heard this too there's  definitely a lot of people who are  trying to get into the industry more  than ever now and you know everyone kind  of wants to be software developers it  seems and sure there's probably a lot of  developers out there who are simply not  ready for full-time jobs and maybe  they're getting hired but they're not  being productive and but you know like  as I said there's plenty of examples to  show out there are people who are  totally killing it in the industry  without a degree the main thing to  understand though is that it's not easy  it just takes a lot of hard work and  there's really just no shortcuts not  getting a degree it doesn't mean that  you take a shortcut it simply means that  you work really really really extra  extra hard to kind of make up for that  lack of degree right you can't just  expect to maybe take a course online or  attend to cheat three months boot camp  or take like you know like an online  boot camp like Udacity or something and  then all of a sudden magically have a  job you can do all those things but  that's just one part of the process and  in addition to that you have to put  hours and hours of practice and work and  and really just make up for that time  that you didn't spend in school getting  a degree and so to not be one of those  people who are you know not being  productive in the industry you just need  to go above and beyond and every person  I know who's successful in the industry  that doesn't have a degree they're  successful because they've put these  hours and dedicated their lives to  making this reality come true  so again if you're willing to put the  hard work then you honestly just won't  be one of these developers who's not  productive I actually have never met  someone who's worked that hard and and  and like didn't get better over time or  wasn't the one of the better best  programmers I've ever met right and even  if it takes you longer to get there than  some others just don't compare yourself  to other people it's just  about putting in the hours putting in  the time practicing coding getting  projects on your under your belt like  you know it's frustrating like the first  few months are very frustrating just  keep trying keep trying and overtime  like you'll look you'll look up and  you're like oh my god I just know so  much how did I get how did I even get  here right and I think if I have to go  back and think one of the hardest part  about not taking this path of not  getting your degree is probably getting  your first interviews and getting your  first job because you don't have you  don't have any experience you don't have  anything to show that you you can call  it basically and so what I would do like  as I said in my last video if you don't  have something like if you don't have a  degree if we don't have experience don't  focus on what you don't have show them  everything else that you do got so this  can be this can be like this can be like  side projects or this can be like  internships or this can be like open  source work that you've done or blog  post that you've written you just make  up for that lack of degree in other ways  right and another thing I want empathize  is if you choose to not get a degree one  thing that happened to me was I noticed  that like I was starting to I really  want to fill in my gaps of some of the  formal fundamental CS stuff like things  like computer networking operating  systems compilers algorithms and data  structures design patterns like these  are things I still wanted to learn  because I think those are very very  important to if we were gonna be a  serious offer engineer these are all  important things to learn and so what I  did was I would learn these things on  the side on weekends or nights just like  picking up a few books and just like  learning learning them on my own because  I really wanted to fill in those gaps  that I didn't get through formal  training and I actually have met with a  few companies now that are actually um  specifically targeting people who don't  have CS degrees but are software  engineers and they're teaching more of  these CS fundamentals like I met her the  school called Bradfield school of  computer science in San Francisco and  they teach things like you know computer  networking computer architecture  databases compilers so you know like  find something like this to fill in the  gaps if you don't get that formal  training and instead if you take more of  a online course or a bootcamp  and lastly the the writer who wrote in  said he wants to be a C++ developer  without a CS degree or is he's asking if  he should get a CS degree to be a C++  developer obviously I can't answer that  question cuz I've never tried that  myself I was a JavaScript developer and  I also did some API work and back and  work like I slowly moved down the stack  but my gut tells me is definitely  possible  perhaps there's less C++ content a lot  more content to learn online with  JavaScript Ruby or Python but I think  anything and anything in the world is  possible without a degree so if you if  you're confident that you can do it I  don't think you I'm never gonna say that  you must get a degree to do anything  because I just don't believe that but I  do want to say that just because I  didn't go back to get a CS agree because  it doesn't by any means mean that I  think a CS degree is a waste of time in  fact if I was in high school and I knew  what I know now about computer science  I'd probably do the computer science  degree over the systems engineering  degree that I did but you know like no  regrets at the time when I was in you  know 18 I really fell in love with  systems engineering  I loved my major so I have no regrets  it's just that I learned about computer  science a little bit later in life so I  didn't have a chance to take CS in  college but I think ICS degree if you're  still in high school is obviously  incredibly valuable and should totally  do it if you're in high school but it's  not game over if you haven't if it's too  late there's always ways to to learn how  to be a developer like I dated many  others have done so I want to close off  by saying if you want to be a developer  you just need to start doing you just  need to start building because being a  developer is not about sitting in a  classroom or taking exams or taking  tests or getting certificates or getting  a degree it's really about just building  software and if you can prove to people  that you can build software the degree  is just a tiny part of it and that will  soon be irrelevant in in the future and  and as I said there's just been  thousands of people who've been  successful without the degree and it  really just depends on how much effort  work and hard work you're willing to put  into this so yeah  that's kind of my rationale for why I  didn't take that path but I hope you  found this helpful and I'll see you in  next week's video
 

